### Configure Playwright Web Server with Basic Options (TypeScript/JavaScript)

Source: https://github.com/microsoft/playwright/blob/v1.58.2/docs/src/test-webserver-js.md

This configuration snippet for `playwright.config.ts` demonstrates how to set up Playwright's `webServer` option. It defines the command to start a local development server, the URL to monitor for readiness, and options like `reuseExistingServer` for efficient test execution. This ensures a development server is active before tests begin, particularly useful for local development workflows.

```javascript
import { defineConfig } from '@playwright/test';

export default defineConfig({
  // Run your local dev server before starting the tests
  webServer: {
    command: 'npm run start',
    url: 'http://localhost:3000',
    reuseExistingServer: !process.env.CI,
    stdout: 'ignore',
    stderr: 'pipe',
  },
});
```

--------------------------------

### Configure Web Server Launch in Playwright Config

Source: https://github.com/microsoft/playwright/blob/v1.58.2/docs/src/release-notes-js.md

Configures automatic web server launch before running tests using the webServer option in playwright.config.ts. The server waits for the specified URL to be available before tests run, and the URL is passed as baseURL to Playwright context.

```typescript
import { defineConfig } from '@playwright/test';
export default defineConfig({
  webServer: {
    command: 'npm run start',
    url: 'http://127.0.0.1:3000',
    timeout: 120 * 1000,
    reuseExistingServer: !process.env.CI,
  },
});
```

--------------------------------

### Config with retries, trace, and webServer

Source: https://github.com/microsoft/playwright/blob/main/packages/web/playwright.config.ts

Full config showing retries: process.env.CI ? 2 : 0, trace: 'on-first-retry', and a single webServer with reuseExistingServer. The CI install command for Chromium is npx playwright install chromium (from docs/src/test-cli-js.md).

```typescript
import path from 'path';
import { devices, defineConfig } from '@playwright/test';

process.env.PWTEST_UNDER_TEST = '1';

const outputDir = path.join(__dirname, '..', '..', 'test-results');

export default defineConfig({
  testDir: 'src',
  outputDir,
  forbidOnly: !!process.env.CI,
  retries: process.env.CI ? 2 : 0,
  reporter: process.env.CI ? [
    ['dot'],
    ['json', { outputFile: path.join(outputDir, 'report.json') }],
    ['blob', { outputDir: path.join(__dirname, '..', '..', 'blob-report') }],
    ['../../tests/config/parquetReporter.ts'],
  ] : [
    ['html', { open: 'on-failure' }]
  ],
  tag: process.env.PW_TAG,
  use: {
    baseURL: 'http://localhost:3102/playwright/gallery/index.html',
    serviceWorkers: 'block',
    reuseContext: true,
    trace: 'on-first-retry',
  },
  projects: [{
    name: 'chromium',
    use: { ...devices['Desktop Chrome'] },
  }],
  webServer: {
    command: 'npx vite --config playwright/vite.config.ts',
    url: 'http://localhost:3102/playwright/gallery/index.html',
    reuseExistingServer: !process.env.CI,
  },
});
```

--------------------------------

### Web-First Screenshot Assertions in Playwright

Source: https://github.com/microsoft/playwright/blob/v1.58.2/docs/src/release-notes-js.md

Uses web-first screenshot assertions that wait for screenshot stabilization before capturing. The assertion automatically disables animations and uses CSS scale, improving test reliability and consistency for visual regression testing.

```javascript
await page.goto('https://playwright.dev');
await expect(page).toHaveScreenshot();
```

### Version 1.22 > Highlights > Web-First Screenshot Assertions

Source: https://github.com/microsoft/playwright/blob/v1.58.2/docs/src/release-notes-js.md

Web-first screenshot assertions automatically wait for screenshot stabilization before capturing, enhancing test reliability and reducing flaky tests. These assertions include screenshot-specific defaults such as disabling animations and using CSS scale options to ensure consistent visual comparisons. Screenshots are saved at the same location as snapshot files, integrating seamlessly with Playwright's snapshot testing workflow.

